  "Question": "The graph of \\(y=\\log x\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "Cuts the \\(y\\)-axis",
    "B": "Cuts all lines perpendicular to the \\(x\\)-axis",
    "C": "Cuts the \\(x\\)-axis",
    "D": "Cuts neither axis",
    "E": "Cuts all circles whose center is at the origin"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "A point on the \\(x\\)-axis has \\(y=0\\), so look for an input whose logarithm is zero. For any valid logarithm base \\(a>0\\), \\(a\\ne1\\), we have \\(a^0=1\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\log_a1=0.\n\\]\nThe graph passes through \\((1,0)\\), so it cuts the \\(x\\)-axis. Its real domain is \\(x>0\\), which also shows why it cannot cut the \\(y\\)-axis or every vertical line. The answer is choice C.",
